## Evidence boundary

The code record establishes the consequences a grid operator may impose after repeated GTO breaches, but it does not allocate civil damages among the hub, a member, an installer and a software provider. ACM says that allocation is outside its power. No primary source found establishes that a blocked business park will pay the proposed diagnostic or recurring price; that an installer will preserve a separately priced wholesale line; or that a group will grant the required operating and evidence rights. Those questions remain the paid-test gates. Programme budgets and contract availability are not evidence of customer payment.
# Essay 06 sol-pass notes

## Evidence corrections

- GTO: final ACM decision published 19 December 2025. Applications are possible now; the operator duty to offer begins 1 January 2027. The essay distinguishes those dates.
- Alternative rights: the ACM decision was announced 19 July 2024 and enabled regional time-block products from 1 April 2025. Enexis confirms its Blokstroom application route began then, but its fully variable Reststroom remains under investigation.
- GTO breach: the final decision permits notice, suspension after repeated breaches and eventual lapse after another breach following remedies.
- Civil liability: ACM expressly did not allocate it. The essay treats member/installer/software fault allocation as a contract term for the paid test.
- Enervalis: the accessible own material supports only the portal slogan. No feature or outcome is attributed.

## Commercial gates left for the experiment

- Whether a blocked anchor site will pay the diagnostic and recurring design prices.
- Whether an installer will preserve a separately priced wholesale layer.
- Whether a group will grant control, evidence and incident-review rights.
- Whether two engineers can safely operate fifteen sites without founder judgement at each peak.

All economics are design hurdles. Working capital, capex, founder replacement labour, owner distribution and retained surplus are separate.

## AGENT.md pre-ship walk

1. Buyer, event, substitute: blocked anchor member; deferred investment; installer EMS, trader/VPP, consultant or manual coordination.
2. Demand boundary: public support and contract availability are separated from willingness to pay.
3. Economics: founder replacement labour, owner distribution, working capital, capex and retained cash are distinct.
4. Twentieth delivery: partner-led acquisition, connector kit, test protocol, implementation engineer and recurring exception work.
5. Margin capture: installer internalisation, trader bundling, operator tools and customer procurement are explicit.
6. Language: installer/adviser owns Dutch workshops, discovery and on-call discussion; founder does not become the Dutch energy manager.
7. Ninety days: €6,000 paid baseline; €15,000 maximum pre-evidence spend; explicit pass and kill rules.
8. Receipts: 16 verified rows have 16 excerpts; one inference is labelled; all arithmetic is a design hurdle.
