    37	<p>The group contract has moved from proposal to code, but availability still needs qualification. On 19 December 2025, ACM said energy-hub companies can apply and grid operators must offer the form from 1 January 2027. <!-- CHECK: 06-gto-final --> The legal route is live; a universal service obligation is not. Neither fact guarantees a useful group limit or approves a control vendor.</p>
    38	<p>Implementation is uneven. Liander describes a current group-contract application route for large connections above 3×80 A, requiring a formed hub, high-frequency metering and all members on the same electricity station. It says at least one member must have contracted transport capacity and gives an average two-year lead time for forming the group and agreeing the contract. <!-- CHECK: 06-liander-route --> Enexis says it is working with ten business parks and expects to offer GTOs more broadly from 2027. <!-- CHECK: 06-enexis-rollout --> An entrant should therefore sell neither “a Dutch GTO product” nor a deadline. It should qualify the operator, station topology, connection class and application status before a software proposal.</p>
    39	<p>Other contract forms solve different operating problems. In a decision announced on 19 July 2024, ACM allowed regional operators to offer time-block transport from 1 April 2025. It also described a TenneT duration-bound right that permits maximum use at least 85% of the time. <!-- CHECK: 06-atr-date --> ACM's 2 June 2026 dashboard separately lists capacity-restriction and capacity-steering contracts, group variants, time-block and duration-bound rights, and a fully non-firm right tied to actual grid loading. <!-- CHECK: 06-contract-taxonomy --></p>
    40	<p>The difference between code and product is visible at Enexis. Its Blokstroom application route for consumption started on 1 April 2025. Its fully variable Reststroom is still under investigation because available capacity cannot yet be seen well enough. <!-- CHECK: 06-enexis-atr-status --> A project needing a firm evening peak cannot be sold either form and called solved.</p>
    41	<p>Capacity-limiting contracts are also concrete. Liander describes a CBC with call-up, a CBC with fixed adjustment times, and redispatch in which a Congestion Service Provider (CSP) bids the customer's flexibility on the GOPACS trading platform. <!-- CHECK: 06-liander-cbc --> An ACM dashboard defines a CBC more narrowly: a company voluntarily and temporarily adjusts load when overload threatens, in exchange for compensation. <!-- CHECK: 06-cbc-compensation --> That duty and payment do not prove that a park will fund a separate evidence layer.</p>
    42	<p>The public response is material but is not customer revenue. Rijksoverheid says €62 million is available for advice and investments for organisations blocked by a full grid, and €166 million for stimulating energy hubs. <!-- CHECK: 06-public-support --> Those are programme budgets, not a price list, award to this company, or proof that an installer will share margin. The demand evidence that matters is narrower: a member has a blocked, costed investment and the group has reached a contract and governance choice that must be operated.</p>
    43	</section>
    44	
    45	<section class="sale">
    46	<h2>The first sale</h2>
    47	<p>Use an actual operator map to build a ten-type prospect list. Include park-management foundations, estates with an approved hub study, and cold-storage, food, logistics, metals, waste and fleet-depot anchor tenants. Include the electrical installer or energy adviser already analysing their profiles. Screen out parks without common station eligibility, a committed anchor investment, or agreement to share interval data. An ambitious solar plan is not enough.</p>
    48	<p>The opening route is through a Dutch-speaking electrical installer and an energy consultant, not independent English-language outbound. They already explain switchgear, meter access, permits, operation limits and member politics. The founder can lead integration design and a transparent evidence model in English; the channel partner must lead Dutch workshops, contractual discovery and on-call discussions. If the partner asks the founder to become the park's Dutch-speaking energy manager, the model violates the reader's constraint and should be declined.</p>
    49	<p>Sell a four-week, <strong>€6,000 paid capacity operating baseline</strong>. That is a required test price, not an observed market rate. It reconciles fifteen-minute data against the group's proposed limit; maps controllable loads and their local owners; rehearses one safe curtailment path in a test window; specifies the command/acknowledgement/evidence log; and produces an exception and liability schedule. It excludes hardware, switchgear changes, protection settings, a grid-operator application, energy-market trading and any promise that the hub will be admitted.</p>
    50	<p>The signers should be the anchor member's investment director and the hub entity or delegated park manager, because a control order reallocates a shared commercial right. The production offer is a required hurdle of <strong>€20,000 onboarding plus €4,500 per live site per month</strong>, billed through the installer under a disclosed wholesale schedule. Hardware and installation remain on the installer's invoice. Before proposing it, require a written allocation policy, a 24/7 contact at every member and a controller test interface. The group must also decide who bears the consequence when a member overrides a curtailment instruction.</p>
    51	</section>
