+<tr><td><strong>Peak cash need</strong></td><td><strong>89.25 + 30.5 + 5 = 124.75</strong></td><td><strong>75.8 + 10 = 85.8, funded from surplus</strong></td></tr>
+<tr><td><strong>€100k capital test</strong></td><td><strong>Fails by 24.75</strong></td><td><strong>Passes; 105.7 remains after reserve and capex</strong></td></tr>
 </tbody></table></div>
-<p>Year one pays replacement-cost founder labour in the model but no owner distribution. It also loses €64,000, so it cannot continue without capital. The mature case pays €110,000 for the founder's operating role, a separate €100,000 owner distribution, and retains €419,000 before tax. That works only if two delivery engineers can run fifteen sites while an on-call electrical-control specialist handles exceptions. The 7.5-site load is a design hurdle. Time-log integrations, tested commands, exceptions, overrides and incident reviews. If each peak needs founder judgement, the margin is fictional.</p>
-<p>The 75% gross-margin hurdle is not licence economics. It pays for integration variance, secure hosting, observability, control testing, audit records, specialist review and a response path. A 25% wholesale discount on the €810,000 recurring line removes €202,500 before costs change. That is why the installer cannot simply be “the channel” and take whatever margin it requests. The wholesale card has to cover responsibilities and show the anchor member an outcome worth buying.</p>
-<p>Working capital is not an expense, so the table shows it separately. The year-one design reserves €70,000 for the operating loss and receivables gap, plus €5,000 for owned test equipment and secure connectivity. Production customers fund site hardware directly. They pay onboarding 50% in advance and recurring service monthly in advance. These are required terms, not observed supplier practice. Do not hire a second engineer, buy a gateway fleet or promise response times before signed receipts fund them.</p>
+<p>Year one pays replacement-cost founder labour but no owner distribution. It loses €89,250. Adding a two-month cash-cost buffer and €5,000 capex puts peak cash at about €124,750. The €100,000 capital ceiling therefore fails by €24,750 under these hurdles. The ninety-day €15,000 cap must sit inside these annual costs, not on top. Continue only if signed terms, volume or lower delivery effort close the gap before hiring.</p>
+<p>The mature case pays €110,000 for the founder's operating role and a separate €100,000 owner distribution. It retains €191,500 before tax. After a €75,800 working-capital reserve and €10,000 capex, €105,700 remains. The 67% gross margin is not licence economics. It funds integration variance, secure telemetry, control testing, audit records, specialist review and response cover.</p>
+<p>Two delivery engineers must each run 7.5 sites as a design hurdle. They handle integrations, tested commands, exceptions, overrides and incident reviews, with an electrical-control specialist covering escalations. If each peak needs founder judgement, the margin is fictional. The installer cannot simply take whatever share it requests: each extra discount point reduces receipts while delivery duties remain.</p>
+<p>Working capital is cash held, not an expense. The hurdle is two months of delivery and operating costs because collections or acceptance can still slip despite required terms. Production customers fund site hardware directly, pay onboarding 50% in advance and pay recurring service monthly in advance. Do not hire a second engineer, buy a gateway fleet or promise response times before signed receipts fund them.</p>
 </section>
 
 <section class="scale">
@@ -83,12 +89,12 @@
 <p><strong>Customer twenty</strong> is possible only through three to five enabled installer/adviser partners. Recurrence follows changing state. Members join or leave, allocations change, equipment is maintained and controllers go offline. New loads change profiles, contract calls change permitted actions, overrides need investigation and group settlement needs evidence. The accumulating asset is a tested library of controller adapters, policy templates, simulator fixtures, failure modes and evidence records. Raw interval data belongs to the member or group under the agreed contracts.</p>
 <p>The four tests are severe. Acquisition repeats only when partners originate qualified baselines without the founder's personal introductions. Delivery is teachable only when someone other than the founder can pass a simulated command-and-recovery test from the playbook. Value recurs only because allocations, equipment and constraints change. The asset accumulates only when the next compatible controller and agreement can be onboarded faster without exposing a prior site's data. If two of these fail, call it specialist integration work and price it accordingly; do not call it a software company.</p>
 <p>Liability starts with the transport right, not the software warranty. Stedin says the group is jointly responsible for staying within capacity and must record its internal allocation in an agreement. It also says an EMS is needed. <!-- CHECK: 06-stedin-hub --> Under ACM's GTO code decision, every breach is reported to the group's representative. Two or more breaches may lead to suspension. A further breach after remedies may ultimately end the transport right. <!-- CHECK: 06-gto-sanctions --></p>
-<p>The code does not say who reimburses a member whose investment stops after a controller failure. ACM expressly declined to set civil-liability terms because it lacks that power. <!-- CHECK: 06-gto-liability --> The hub therefore faces the operator first, then relies on its private contracts. The paid baseline must propose a fault allocation: the member bears deliberate overrides; the installer bears proven hardware or wiring faults; and the software supplier bears proven command or logging defects, subject to a negotiated cap. Lost production, safety and upstream communication need explicit treatment. A Dutch lawyer and insurer must test that schedule before live control.</p>
+<p>The code does not say who reimburses a member whose investment stops after a controller failure. ACM declined to prescribe civil agreements between connected parties or groups and grid operators because it lacked authority to do so. <!-- CHECK: 06-gto-liability --> Allocation among members, the installer and the software supplier is therefore a private-contract design question, not an ACM holding. The paid baseline must propose a fault allocation: the member bears deliberate overrides; the installer bears proven hardware or wiring faults; and the software supplier bears proven command or logging defects, subject to a negotiated cap. Lost production, safety and upstream communication need explicit treatment. A Dutch lawyer and insurer must test that schedule before live control.</p>
 </section>
 
 <section class="competition">
 <h2>The incumbent gets a turn</h2>
-<p>There is no empty category to enter. Sympower markets a stack that connects, aggregates, optimises and trades energy assets, including hardware or API interfaces, for commercial and industrial companies. <!-- CHECK: 06-sympower --> Withthegrid markets Dutch local control strategies including static and dynamic limitations, grid-operator endpoints and peak shaving. <!-- CHECK: 06-withthegrid --> Those are vendor descriptions, not independent evidence of outcomes, but they show that control, integration and market access are already purchasable together.</p>
+<p>There is no empty category to enter. Sympower markets a stack that connects, aggregates, optimises and trades energy assets, including hardware or API interfaces, for commercial and industrial companies. <!-- CHECK: 06-sympower --> Withthegrid markets Dutch local control strategies including static and dynamic limitations, grid-operator endpoints and peak shaving. <!-- CHECK: 06-withthegrid --> Here peak shaving means using batteries to counter short load or generation spikes that would breach a consumption or feed-in limit. <!-- CHECK: 06-peak-shaving --> Those are vendor descriptions, not independent evidence of outcomes, but they show that control, integration and market access are already purchasable together.</p>
 <p>Scholt Energy markets flexibility around renewable generation, storage and industrial consumption. It says its virtual power plant connects those assets to energy markets. <!-- CHECK: 06-scholt --> Enervalis' accessible portal says only “Creating more value with energy.” <!-- CHECK: 06-enervalis --> That is too little own-material evidence to attribute a current control product, outcome or price. <!-- CHECK: 06-enervalis-boundary --> It is not proof that Enervalis has no such capability. It makes vendor discovery necessary before claiming differentiation.</p>
 <p>The grid operators have their own hub programmes. Liander publishes the eligibility route and process. <!-- CHECK: 06-liander-route --> Enexis has ten development sites, expects wider availability in 2027 and requires an EMS. <!-- CHECK: 06-enexis-rollout --> <!-- CHECK: 06-enexis-gto --> Stedin describes the same control need and offers a Netburenscan to identify possible group members. <!-- CHECK: 06-stedin-hub --> The operator cannot be displaced from capacity allocation. An installer can retain the controller, and a trader can bundle dispatch. A park can also buy a consultant and an EMS. Any of them can make a separate evidence layer look redundant.</p>
 <p>The narrow response is neutrality between equipment and market counterparties, not superiority in dispatch. Keep an independent contract-to-control evidence record, support the awkward mixed fleet an installer inherited, and make the installer more credible with its end customer. The partner receives branded reports, first-line relationship ownership and a clear wholesale margin. The new company retains the reconciliation engine, test fixtures, secure audit trail and specialist control policy. If the partner demands the source, the customer data and unrestricted reuse while paying a thin fee, it is buying know-how to internalise.</p>
@@ -110,7 +116,7 @@
 <li><strong>Days 45–70:</strong> offer the same <strong>€6,000 paid baseline</strong>, 50% in advance, to five qualified installer-led groups. It is valid only where an anchor member will expose redacted interval data and a safe controller-test interface.</li>
 <li><strong>Days 70–90:</strong> deliver one baseline and seek the stated €20,000 onboarding plus €4,500 monthly production hurdle through the installer. Total pre-evidence spend is capped at <strong>€15,000</strong>.</li>
 </ol>
-<p><strong>Pass:</strong> one qualified group pays the baseline and completes a witnessed safe-control rehearsal. The anchor member and installer must then sign a production order or dated authority at the stated price hurdles, with written allocation and liability terms. <strong>Kill:</strong> no paid baseline after five qualified offers; no group can name a blocked investment and eligible connection arrangement; or installers will sell only if they own all evidence, customer contact and reusable control work. In any kill state, offer occasional integration consulting only if premium self-employment is acceptable—do not call it the second engine.</p>
+<p><strong>Pass:</strong> one qualified group pays the baseline and completes a witnessed safe-control rehearsal. It must then make one binding commitment: a production order at the €20,000 onboarding and €4,500 monthly retail hurdles, a non-refundable €10,000 onboarding deposit, or a paid €4,500 first month. A letter of interest or dated authority does not count. Written allocation and liability terms must accompany the commitment. <strong>Kill or downgrade:</strong> no paid baseline after five qualified offers; no eligible blocked investment; installer capture of all evidence and reusable work; or a paid baseline without a production commitment by day 90. The last outcome kills the recurring-company thesis and downgrades the offer to occasional integration consulting, if premium self-employment is acceptable.</p>
 </section>
 
