 <p>Implementation is uneven. Liander describes a current group-contract application route for large connections above 3×80 A, requiring a formed hub, high-frequency metering and all members on the same electricity station. It says at least one member must have contracted transport capacity and gives an average two-year lead time for forming the group and agreeing the contract. <!-- CHECK: 06-liander-route --> Enexis says it is working with ten business parks and expects to offer GTOs more broadly from 2027. <!-- CHECK: 06-enexis-rollout --> An entrant should therefore sell neither “a Dutch GTO product” nor a deadline. It should qualify the operator, station topology, connection class and application status before a software proposal.</p>
 <p>Other contract forms solve different operating problems. In a decision announced on 19 July 2024, ACM allowed regional operators to offer time-block transport from 1 April 2025. It also described a TenneT duration-bound right that permits maximum use at least 85% of the time. <!-- CHECK: 06-atr-date --> ACM's 2 June 2026 dashboard separately lists capacity-restriction and capacity-steering contracts, group variants, time-block and duration-bound rights, and a fully non-firm right tied to actual grid loading. <!-- CHECK: 06-contract-taxonomy --></p>
 <p>The difference between code and product is visible at Enexis. Its Blokstroom application route for consumption started on 1 April 2025. Its fully variable Reststroom is still under investigation because available capacity cannot yet be seen well enough. <!-- CHECK: 06-enexis-atr-status --> A project needing a firm evening peak cannot be sold either form and called solved.</p>
-<p>Capacity-limiting contracts are also concrete. Liander describes a CBC with call-up, a CBC with fixed adjustment times, and redispatch through GOPACS via a Congestion Service Provider. <!-- CHECK: 06-liander-cbc --> These forms can create a control duty and a payment mechanism. <!-- CHECK: 06-contract-taxonomy --> They do not prove that a park will fund a separate evidence layer.</p>
+<p>Capacity-limiting contracts are also concrete. Liander describes a CBC with call-up, a CBC with fixed adjustment times, and redispatch in which a Congestion Service Provider (CSP) bids the customer's flexibility on the GOPACS trading platform. <!-- CHECK: 06-liander-cbc --> An ACM dashboard defines a CBC more narrowly: a company voluntarily and temporarily adjusts load when overload threatens, in exchange for compensation. <!-- CHECK: 06-cbc-compensation --> That duty and payment do not prove that a park will fund a separate evidence layer.</p>
 <p>The public response is material but is not customer revenue. Rijksoverheid says €62 million is available for advice and investments for organisations blocked by a full grid, and €166 million for stimulating energy hubs. <!-- CHECK: 06-public-support --> Those are programme budgets, not a price list, award to this company, or proof that an installer will share margin. The demand evidence that matters is narrower: a member has a blocked, costed investment and the group has reached a contract and governance choice that must be operated.</p>
 </section>
 
